I read only the three permitted files. No files were changed and no guest was run. Some broader premises cannot be verified within that scope.

1. **MVME187 installs this device unconditionally within its subtype setup.**

   The installation is at [machine_mvme88k.c:94](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c:94): it constructs `"vme addr=0x%x"` using `VME2_BASE`, then calls `device_add`. This precedes the `prom_emulation` guard at [line 142](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c:142), so disabling PROM emulation does not remove the device.

   The adjacent comment identifies the base as **`0xfff40000`**. Strictly, the executable expression is `VME2_BASE`; its numeric definition is outside the permitted files. The registered length is **`0x1000` bytes**, as established below.

   This is not unconditional installation across the MVME88K family: the MVME188 and MVME197 branches contain only their names and TODOs at [line 127](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c:127). These files establish MVME187 attachment, but cannot establish tree-wide exclusivity or that the project currently boots it successfully.

2. **The aperture contains 4,096 byte offsets; `default:` is the complement of ten exact case values.**

   [`VME_LEN`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:50) is `0x1000`, and [registration](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:164) passes that length and `dev_vme_access`. Thus relative offsets occupy `[0, 0x1000)`. Using the documented base, the physical window is `0xfff40000` through `0xfff40fff`.

   The [switch](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:68) has exactly these ten labels:

   ```text
   C = { 0,
         VME2_GCSRCTL, VME2_TCR, VME2_T1CMP, VME2_T1COUNT,
         VME2_TCTL, VME2_IRQEN, VME2_IRQL3, VME2_IRQL4, VME2_VBR }
   ```

   Let `A` be the aperture’s byte offsets. The exact default set is **`A \ C`**, with cardinality **`4096 − |A ∩ C|`**.

   The important qualification is that none of the nine `VME2_*` values is defined in the permitted files. Consequently:

   | Access-start domain | Available starts | Default count if all ten cases belong to that domain | Bounds established without the missing definitions |
   |---|---:|---:|---:|
   | Every byte offset | 4,096 | **4,086 = 4,096 − 10** | 4,086–4,095 |
   | Naturally aligned 32-bit starts | 1,024 | **1,014 = 1,024 − 10** | 1,014–1,023 |

   These count starting offsets, not hardware registers. The switch compares the **raw byte offset**, although backing storage indexes by `relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)`. A case therefore does not automatically handle all four bytes of its backing word.

   The dispatch excludes individual named addresses wherever they lie; it is not a check for “beyond the last implemented register.” Any gaps between case values also take `default:`. However, the exact numeric intervals—and an unconditional assertion that they are scattered—require the missing constants. If those constants are in-range and word-aligned, the byte-offset defaults are indeed scattered between named starts. I would not present either that condition or the exact 4,086 count as independently verified here.

3. **Source-level reachability exists on an unmodified MVME187 configuration; stock-boot execution of this arm is not established.**

   The registered family alias is **`mvme88k`**, and its MVME187 subtype selector is **`mvme187`**, at [machine_mvme88k.c:204](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c:204). No extra device attachment is needed.

   Once an ordinary mapped access reaches the [handler](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:57), there is no additional feature or operating-mode guard before dispatch. Even restricting consideration to aligned word starts, ten case labels cannot cover the aperture.

   But the explicit firmware-related evidence needs care: [offset zero](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:70) is documented as used by OpenBSD probing, and **that offset is handled successfully**. That comment proves a supported probe exists; it does not prove that an unmodified boot image accesses an unmatched offset.

4. **Promoting the default-arm calls to legacy `fatal()` removes verbosity suppression and does not activate `#261`.**

   Both premises are correct at their specified site:

   - [`debug()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:365) starts with `v = verbose`, subtracts one during emulation, adds one when stepping or about to step, then returns when `(quiet_mode && !ss) || v < 0`.
   - [`fatal()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:384) has no filtering early-out and calls `va_debug` directly.

   With the initialized `verbose = 0`, normal execution and no stepping produce `v = -1`. Thus `debug()` suppresses the message even without quiet mode, before the [default arm’s `exit(1)`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:137) ends the process. “Invisible at default verbosity” is therefore correct **during normal execution**; startup and debugger contexts can behave differently.

   [`va_debug()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:118) formats and prints directly. It neither terminates the process nor routes through structured subsystem logging.

   The `#261` check instead resides in [`debugmsg_va()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:146), where the global toggle and message severity determine whether to request debugger entry. Legacy `fatal()` supplies no structured ERROR severity and bypasses this path entirely—even when the toggle is armed. The `fatal()` call inside the breakpoint branch merely prints its announcement; the surrounding branch sets `about_to_enter_single_step`.

   **One gate-relevant qualification:** `va_debug()` uses `printf`, so its destination is **stdout**, and it performs no explicit flush. Promotion ensures the message is not filtered; it does not guarantee immediate delivery through a buffered pipe. A gate must capture stdout and account for flushing. Immediate delivery before guest completion would require a separate flushing guarantee.

   Also, the named-offset premise is slightly overstated: there are **nine active nonfatal diagnostic calls across five named offsets**—GCSRCTL, TCR, IRQEN, IRQL3 and IRQL4. The TCTL notice is commented out at [dev_vme.c:103](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:103). Counting that comment and the two default-arm calls produces twelve textual calls, not twelve active nonfatal named-offset notices.

5. **The latch belongs in `struct vme_data`; this file currently has no announce latch of either kind.**

   The [structure](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:52) contains only `reg[]`. Each initialization separately allocates and zeroes the entire structure at [line 157](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:157), and registration passes that instance as the handler’s `extra`.

   Add one instance field, shared by both default-arm directions and every unmatched offset. Its meaning should be “this instance has announced an unsupported register.” Existing whole-structure zeroing initializes it. Named cases must not consume it.

   That implements **one announcement total per instance lifetime**, with `(once)` explicitly present in the message. Separate read/write latches would permit two announcements; per-offset latches would permit many; a function-static latch would let one instance suppress another.

   There is no applicable latch population in these three files from which to re-derive the tree-wide spelling plurality. Here, **N = 1 and `(once)` are your stated disposition requirements**, not independently verified repository statistics.

6. **The oracle needs independent evidence of access, guest continuation, and exact diagnostic identity and count.**

   First make the decline’s data behavior explicit. With bus faults excluded, “decline” means refusing to implement the register while completing through the normal [successful return](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:146). A concrete conservative fallback is ignored writes and zero reads, documented as emulator behavior rather than verified hardware semantics.

   **Deleting `exit(1)` alone does not guarantee zero reads.** The handler has already loaded `odata` from `d->reg[]` at [line 65](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:65). An unmatched byte offset can share a backing slot with a named offset. A zero-read fallback therefore needs an explicit default-arm override.

   For post-repair validation, record independent handler/default-branch coverage, including the first actual offset and direction. Require subsequent guest progress after repeated completed accesses, the chosen read result, and unchanged register state for declined writes. A successful boot banner or exit status alone is insufficient.

   | Outcome | Distinguishing evidence |
   |---|---|
   | Correct decline | Repeated default-arm access is independently observed; guest continues; exactly one default-arm announcement per instance contains `(once)` and identifies the first actual access correctly. |
   | Silent decline | Access and continuation are established, but the announcement count is zero. |
   | Announcement every time | More than one default-arm announcement occurs for the same instance. |
   | One announcement for the wrong offset | Total count is one, but its offset or direction disagrees with the independently recorded first default access. |
   | Access never happened | Default-arm coverage is absent, regardless of apparent guest success or unrelated output. |

   For writes, also check the diagnostic’s reported write data against the tested register-width value. Count **all** default-arm announcements, not merely lines matching the expected offset.

   Cover fresh instances with read-first and write-first activity, mixed directions and offsets, and two device instances in the same process. Named accesses before the first default access must leave the allowance available. Verify default verbosity and quiet mode, with `#261` both disabled and armed; the promoted announcement must not request debugger entry. A separate structured-ERROR positive control can establish that the armed gate actually works.

   These are validation requirements, not results from a test performed here.

7. **Only (b) matches the ratified disposition.**

   | Candidate | Matches? | Hardware meaning and cost |
   |---|---|---|
   | **(a) Delete `exit(1)`, retain `debug()`** | **No.** | Allows completion without implementing the register. Writes remain ignored and reads retain the preloaded backing value. The edit is tiny, but default-execution silence remains; visible diagnostics can repeat without bound. |
   | **(b) Delete `exit(1)`, promote to `fatal()` with an instance latch** | **Yes**, including `(once)` and successful completion. | Makes incomplete emulation observable without asserting a bus fault or inventing writable-register semantics. Costs one state field, a guard on default accesses, and at most one announcement per instance. Define the read fallback explicitly. Legacy `fatal()` does not activate `#261`. |
   | **(c) Return zero from the access function** | **No; explicitly ruled out.** | Under your settled M88K caller behavior, reports a failed transaction and produces a guest bus exception. That asserts a hardware failure response unsupported here and can divert or stop the guest’s execution. The caller behavior is accepted from your adjudication; `memory_rw.c` was outside this review. |
   | **(d) Store writes and read back `d->reg[]`** | **No.** | Invents persistent, RAM-like register semantics. Firmware can then rely on fabricated state while actual controller behavior remains absent. The array already exists, so the primary cost is semantic and maintenance burden rather than a new register allocation. |

   Finally, “as the named cases do” needs narrowing: only **T1CMP, T1COUNT, TCTL and VBR** explicitly store writes. The five named diagnostic cases do not. Moreover, [T1COUNT](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_vme.c:90) advances its stored count by 100 on reads, so even those named cases do not share one uniform read/write-storage behavior.
